request.description:
Liveness probe on madlad-api (the proxy container). Returns {"status": "ok"} once the FastAPI app has started — does NOT verify madlad-app (the inference container) is reachable, since the model is lazy-loaded on the first /translate or /languages call. Docker's own healthcheck for madlad-api polls this endpoint. See MADLAD.md § Health check.

#### Schemas

Response `200` (`application/json`) — madlad-api is up; madlad-app is not checked:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": ["status"],
  "properties": {
    "status": {"const": "ok"}
  }
}
```

List the ~400 target languages MADLAD-400 supports. Response shape: {"languages": [{"code": "af", "name": "Afrikaans"}, {"code": "am", "name": "Amharic"}, …]}. Codes are ISO 639-1 where available, ISO 639-3 for low-resource languages, occasionally with a regional suffix (e.g. `zh_CN`). Names are resolved via the `langcodes` library. Use a returned `code` as `target_lang` in POST /translate.

First call after container start triggers the lazy model download (~1-3 min for the ~3 GB CT2 checkpoint). Subsequent calls are instant. Proxied to madlad-app's /languages inside the ai_shared network; returns 502 with `Cannot reach madlad-app: …` if the inference container is down or fails (5xx); a 4xx of its own is passed through with its status and `detail`. See MADLAD.md § Supported languages.

#### Schemas

madlad-app's body, passed through. A 4xx of madlad-app's is passed through with its status and `detail`; madlad-app unreachable or a 5xx of its own is a 502 `{"detail": "Cannot reach madlad-app: …"}`.

Response `200` (`application/json`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": ["languages"],
  "properties": {
    "languages": {
      "type": "array",
      "description": "Every target language the checkpoint's tokenizer has a `<2xx>` token for, sorted by code",
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "required": ["code", "name"],
        "properties": {
          "code": {
            "type": "string",
            "pattern": "^[a-z]{2,3}(?:_[A-Za-z]+)?$",
            "description": "Used as `target_lang` in /translate. ISO 639-1 where available, otherwise ISO 639-3, occasionally with a regional suffix (e.g. `zh_CN`)"
          },
          "name": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Resolved via the `langcodes` library; an unknown code falls back to the code itself"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Translate a single string. Body: {"text": string, "target_lang": string}. Source language is auto-detected by MADLAD — there is no `src` field. `target_lang` must be one of the codes returned by GET /languages (ISO 639-1 preferred, e.g. `es`, `fr`, `ja`, `zh`, `de`, `pt`, `ar`, `hi`, `ko`).

Response shape: {"translated": "<string>"}. First call after container start triggers the ~1-3 min lazy load. Steady-state latency is dominated by the model forward pass; the beam-search + repetition guards default to ~3-4× a greedy decode (see MADLAD.md § Decoding parameters for the current beam_size=4 / repetition_penalty=1.1 / no_repeat_ngram_size=3 / max_decoding_length=1024 defaults and when to tune).

Errors: 400 for an unknown `target_lang` (madlad-app's own `Unknown target_lang '<code>'. Use /languages to list supported codes.`, passed through); 422 (FastAPI's) for a body that is not JSON (`json_invalid`) or a missing field; 502 with `Cannot reach madlad-app: …` if the inference container is unreachable or fails (5xx). See MADLAD.md § Usage.

#### Schemas

madlad-app's body, passed through. An unknown `target_lang` is madlad-app's 400 `{"detail": "Unknown target_lang '<code>'. Use /languages to list supported codes."}`, passed through with that status and detail (any madlad-app 4xx is); madlad-app unreachable or a 5xx -- a model that produced nothing is its 500 -- is a 502 `{"detail": "Cannot reach madlad-app: …"}`. A body that is not JSON is FastAPI's 422 (`json_invalid`), as is a missing field.

Request body (`application/json`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": true,
  "required": ["text", "target_lang"],
  "properties": {
    "text": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Source language is auto-detected; there is no `src` field"
    },
    "target_lang": {"type": "string", "description": "One of the codes GET /languages returns"}
  }
}
```

Response `200` (`application/json`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": ["translated"],
  "properties": {
    "translated": {"type": "string"}
  }
}
```

MCP handshake. Response headers include `mcp-session-id` — copy it into the `mcpSessionId` collection variable. Response body is a JSON-RPC result advertising FastMCP's server info + capabilities.

#### Schemas

The JSON-RPC response's `result` (streamable HTTP at `/mcp/`: one SSE `data:` message when the request accepts `text/event-stream`); the `mcp-session-id` response header names the session the later calls send back.

Params (`params`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": true,
  "$comment": "mcp.types.InitializeRequestParams: the SDK allows extra keys",
  "required": ["protocolVersion", "capabilities", "clientInfo"],
  "properties": {
    "protocolVersion": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The latest version of the Model Context Protocol that the client supports"
    },
    "capabilities": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": true},
    "clientInfo": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": true,
      "required": ["name", "version"],
      "properties": {
        "name": {"type": "string"},
        "version": {"type": "string"}
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Result (`result`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": true,
  "$comment": "mcp.types.InitializeResult: the SDK allows extra keys",
  "required": ["protocolVersion", "capabilities", "serverInfo"],
  "properties": {
    "protocolVersion": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The version of the Model Context Protocol that the server wants to use; it may not match the version the client requested"
    },
    "capabilities": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": true,
      "required": ["tools"],
      "description": "What the server supports (`mcp.types.ServerCapabilities`); FastMCP announces `tools`, `prompts`, `resources` and `logging`"
    },
    "serverInfo": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": true,
      "required": ["name", "version"],
      "properties": {
        "name": {"const": "MADLAD Translation"},
        "version": {"type": "string", "description": "The version FastMCP reports"}
      }
    },
    "instructions": {"type": "string"}
  }
}
```

Enumerate available tools. madlad-api exposes exactly one: `translate(text: str, target_lang: str) -> str`. `text` is the source (any language, auto-detected); `target_lang` is an ISO 639-1 code (see GET /languages). Returns the translated string, or an `Error translating: …` string on upstream failure — madlad-app's own message for an unknown `target_lang`, the httpx error when madlad-app is unreachable or fails (the MCP tool does not raise; the error is the return value).

#### Schemas

The JSON-RPC response's `result` (one SSE `data:` message).

Params (`params`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": true,
  "$comment": "mcp.types.PaginatedRequestParams",
  "properties": {
    "cursor": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "An opaque token representing the current pagination position"
    }
  }
}
```

Result (`result`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": true,
  "$comment": "mcp.types.ListToolsResult: the SDK allows extra keys",
  "required": ["tools"],
  "properties": {
    "tools": {
      "type": "array",
      "minItems": 1,
      "maxItems": 1,
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "additionalProperties": true,
        "$comment": "mcp.types.Tool: the SDK allows extra keys",
        "required": ["name", "inputSchema"],
        "properties": {
          "name": {"const": "translate"},
          "description": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The tool's docstring summary: what the model reads"
          },
          "inputSchema": {
            "type": "object",
            "additionalProperties": true,
            "description": "A JSON Schema: the arguments of `MCP translate`"
          },
          "outputSchema": {
            "type": "object",
            "additionalProperties": true,
            "description": "A JSON Schema of `structuredContent`: `{\"result\": <string>}` (FastMCP wraps a `str` return)"
          },
          "_meta": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": true, "description": "FastMCP's own"}
        }
      }
    },
    "nextCursor": {"type": "string"}
  }
}
```

Invoke the `translate` tool via MCP. Result body has a text-content block containing the translated string (e.g. `おはよう` for the example). Equivalent in effect to POST /translate — same lazy-load behavior on the first call, same beam-search decoding defaults. Difference is transport: this path is what chat models routed through LiteLLM's mcp_servers.madlad_translate hit when they decide to translate mid-conversation. See MADLAD.md § LiteLLM integration for the chat-completions → tool-call round-trip pattern.

#### Schemas

The JSON-RPC response's `result.structuredContent` (one SSE `data:` message); `result.content[0].text` carries the same string. A madlad-app failure is not a tool error: it comes back as the string.

Arguments (`params.arguments` of `tools/call`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "$comment": "translate(text: str, target_lang: str) as FastMCP 3.4.5 lists it (it closes a tool's arguments)",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": ["text", "target_lang"],
  "properties": {
    "text": {"type": "string", "description": "The source text. Source language is auto-detected."},
    "target_lang": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "ISO 639-1 language code (e.g. \"es\", \"fr\", \"ja\", \"zh\").\nCall the /languages endpoint for the full list of supported codes."
    }
  }
}
```

Result (`result.structuredContent`):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": ["result"],
  "properties": {
    "result": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The translated text; `Error translating: <madlad-app's detail>` for a 4xx of madlad-app's (an unknown target_lang); `Error translating: <httpx error>` when madlad-app cannot be reached or answers a 5xx. FastMCP wraps a `str` return as `{\"result\": …}`"
    }
  }
}
```
